# FABLE-SONNET-LAST-OCCURRENCE-20260924-164: the author's rule (Q22.2, LMX_blog/2026-09-24.md,
# book Sfields, CORE S3.1) -- an unqualified name is its LAST occurrence, including repeated
# occurrences of the same name in one method body. `test\[N]x` counts in lexical (source) order;
# unqualified `test\x` is `test\[lastIndex]x`.  Declared occurrences, read and written through a
# method root: unit_q24_repeated_decl.lm2.
#
# bafca4c (the author, 2026-09-28: assignment is not a declaration; an argument is its parameter): this
# row numbered the bare assignments of the argument `arg`, one cell under Q29 (`arg: 1`, poke9 through
# @: arg, `arg: 2` -- test\[0]arg and test\arg 2, then 5 after `test\arg: 5`).  They declare nothing now,
# and test\[0]arg -- a place of declaration opened from another method, check (book §11) -- is refused
# where it stands ("no such occurrence"): an argument has none.
fn: poke9 (@: int p) int
    \p: 9
    return: 0
end: poke9

fn: test (int: arg) int
    arg: 1
    poke9(@: arg)
    arg: 2
    return: 0
end: test

fn: check () int
    int: first
    int: last
    test(0)
    first: test\[0]arg
    last: test\arg
    if: first != 2
        return: 81
    if: last != 2
        return: 83
    test\arg: 5
    first: test\[0]arg
    if: first != 5
        return: 84
    return: 7
end: check

sendMessage: exit(exit_code: check(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
